  {
   "xml_index": 6,
   "xml_name": "L03_006_Echo Until Quit",
   "display_name": "Echo Until Quit",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Let's write a very different kind of <code>while</code> loop: one that keeps running <b>until the user tells it to stop</b>, rather than counting up or down a fixed number of times. This pattern is often called a <b>sentinel-controlled loop</b>, because a special value (the \"sentinel\", here the letter <code>q</code>) signals when to quit.</p>\n<pre>text = input()\nwhile text != \"q\":\n    print(\"You entered: \" + text)\n    text = input()</pre>\n<p>Let's trace through this: we read a line first, <i>before</i> the loop even starts. Then, as long as what we read is not <code>\"q\"</code>, we print it back and read another line. As soon as the user types exactly <code>q</code>, the condition <code>text != \"q\"</code> becomes False, and the loop stops immediately - notice we do <b>not</b> print anything for the <code>q</code> itself.</p>\n<p>This is a common and very useful pattern: read once before the loop (to have something to check), then read again as the very last step inside the loop body, so each pass through the loop always starts with a freshly-read value.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> Repeatedly read a line of text. For each line that is not exactly <code>q</code>, print <code>You entered: &lt;text&gt;</code>. As soon as a line is exactly <code>q</code>, stop the loop (and print nothing for that final <code>q</code> line).</p>\n<p><b>Example:</b> Input: <pre>apple\nbanana\nq</pre> Output: <pre>You entered: apple\nYou entered: banana</pre></p>\n<p><b>Edge case (quit immediately):</b> Input: <pre>q</pre> Output: <i>(nothing printed)</i></p>",
   "answerpreload": "",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
