   "xml_name": "L03_008_Range Default Start",
   "display_name": "Range Default Start",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>When you call <code>range()</code> with just <b>one</b> argument, like <code>range(n)</code>, Python assumes you want to start counting from <b>0</b> by default, and stop just before <code>n</code>.</p>\n<pre>for i in range(5):\n    print(i)</pre>\n<p>This prints <code>0, 1, 2, 3, 4</code> - five numbers in total, starting at 0 and stopping right before 5. This is a very common pattern when you just want to repeat something <code>n</code> times and don't especially care what the counter values themselves represent.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> Read a whole number <code>N</code>. Using <code>for i in range(N):</code>, print every value of <code>i</code> that the loop takes, each on its own line (so, the numbers 0 to N-1).</p>\n<p><b>Example:</b> Input: <pre>5</pre> Output: <pre>0\n1\n2\n3\n4</pre></p>\n<p><b>Edge case (N is 1 - only one value, 0):</b> Input: <pre>1</pre> Output: <pre>0</pre></p>",
