  {
   "xml_index": 19,
   "xml_name": "L03_019_Simple N By N Square",
   "display_name": "Simple N By N Square",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>You can put one <code>for</code> loop <b>inside</b> another - this is called a <b>nested loop</b>, and it's how we print two-dimensional patterns like grids and shapes.</p>\n<pre>n = int(input())\nfor row in range(n):\n    for col in range(n):\n        print(\"*\", end=\"\")\n    print()</pre>\n<p>The <b>outer</b> loop runs once per row. For <b>each</b> row, the <b>inner</b> loop runs completely, printing <code>n</code> stars in a row. Notice <code>print(\"*\", end=\"\")</code> - the <code>end=\"\"</code> tells Python not to move to a new line after each star (its normal behavior), so all the stars in one row appear next to each other. Then, after the inner loop finishes, a plain <code>print()</code> (with nothing in it) moves us to a new line, ready for the next row.</p>\n<p><b>Indentation with nesting:</b> the inner <code>for</code> is indented once inside the outer <code>for</code>, and the inner loop's own body is indented <i>again</i> relative to that. The final <code>print()</code> that starts a new line is indented only <b>once</b> (aligned with the inner <code>for</code>), so it runs once per row, after the inner loop completes - not once per star!</p>\n<p><b>Trace through (n=3):</b> Row 0: inner loop prints <code>*</code> three times -&gt; <code>***</code>, then newline. Same for rows 1 and 2. Result: three rows of <code>***</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> Read a whole number <code>n</code> (between 1 and 5). Print an <code>n</code> by <code>n</code> square made of stars, with <code>n</code> stars on each of <code>n</code> rows.</p>\n<p><b>Example (n=3):</b> Output: <pre>***\n***\n***</pre></p>\n<p><b>Edge case (n=1):</b> Output: <pre>*</pre></p>",
   "answerpreload": "",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
