  {
   "xml_index": 20,
   "xml_name": "L03_020_Right Angle Triangle",
   "display_name": "Right Angle Triangle",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Let's build a classic pattern: a right-angle triangle of stars, where row <code>r</code> (counting from 1) has exactly <code>r</code> stars.</p>\n<pre>n = int(input())\nfor row in range(1, n + 1):\n    for col in range(row):\n        print(\"*\", end=\"\")\n    print()</pre>\n<p>The key idea: the <b>inner</b> loop's range depends on the <b>outer</b> loop's current value! On row 1, the inner loop runs <code>range(1)</code> - just 1 star. On row 2, it runs <code>range(2)</code> - 2 stars. And so on, up to row <code>n</code>, which prints <code>n</code> stars.</p>\n<p><b>Trace through (n=3):</b> Row 1: 1 star -&gt; <code>*</code>. Row 2: 2 stars -&gt; <code>**</code>. Row 3: 3 stars -&gt; <code>***</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> Read a whole number <code>n</code> (between 1 and 5). Print a right-angle triangle of stars with <code>n</code> rows, where row <code>r</code> has exactly <code>r</code> stars.</p>\n<p><b>Example (n=3):</b> Output: <pre>*\n**\n***</pre></p>\n<p><b>Edge case (n=1):</b> Output: <pre>*</pre></p>",
   "answerpreload": "",
   "mark": 1.0,
