  {
   "xml_index": 21,
   "xml_name": "L03_021_Horizontally Flipped Triangle",
   "display_name": "Horizontally Flipped Triangle",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Let's flip the right-angle triangle from the previous question <b>horizontally</b> - so instead of the stars starting at the left edge, they end at the right edge, with spaces filling in the empty space on the left of each row.</p>\n<pre>n = int(input())\nfor row in range(1, n + 1):\n    for space in range(n - row):\n        print(\" \", end=\"\")\n    for col in range(row):\n        print(\"*\", end=\"\")\n    print()</pre>\n<p>Each row now has <b>two</b> inner loops run one after another: first, we print some spaces (<code>n - row</code> of them, so earlier rows get more padding), then we print the stars (<code>row</code> of them), then move to a new line.</p>\n<p><b>Trace through (n=3):</b> Row 1: <code>3-1=2</code> spaces, then 1 star -&gt; <code>\"  *\"</code>. Row 2: <code>3-2=1</code> space, then 2 stars -&gt; <code>\" **\"</code>. Row 3: <code>3-3=0</code> spaces, then 3 stars -&gt; <code>\"***\"</code>. Notice all rows line up on the <b>right</b> edge.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> Read a whole number <code>n</code> (between 1 and 5). Print a right-aligned triangle of stars with <code>n</code> rows, as shown above.</p>\n<p><b>Example (n=3):</b> Output: <pre>  *\n **\n***</pre></p>\n<p><b>Edge case (n=1):</b> Output: <pre>*</pre></p>",
   "answerpreload": "",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
