  {
   "xml_index": 28,
   "xml_name": "L03_028_Sum Of Grid Entries",
   "display_name": "Sum Of Grid Entries",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Let's finish the nested-loop section by combining a grid with an accumulator: instead of printing every value in a grid, add them all up into a single total.</p>\n<pre>n = int(input())\ntotal = 0\nfor row in range(1, n + 1):\n    for col in range(1, n + 1):\n        total = total + (row * col)\nprint(total)</pre>\n<p>This uses the exact same nested structure as the multiplication grid, but instead of printing each <code>row * col</code>, we add it into <code>total</code>. The accumulator pattern works the same way inside nested loops as it did inside a single loop - it just gets updated more often (once per grid cell, rather than once per row).</p>\n<p><b>Trace through (n=2):</b> Cells visited: <code>1*1=1</code>, <code>1*2=2</code>, <code>2*1=2</code>, <code>2*2=4</code>. Sum: <code>1+2+2+4 = 9</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> Read a whole number <code>n</code> (between 1 and 5). Compute and print the sum of every entry in the <code>n</code> by <code>n</code> multiplication grid (where the entry at row <code>r</code>, column <code>c</code> is <code>r * c</code>).</p>\n<p><b>Example:</b> Input: <pre>2</pre> Output: <pre>9</pre></p>\n<p><b>Edge case (n=1 - grid is just the single cell 1*1):</b> Input: <pre>1</pre> Output: <pre>1</pre></p>",
   "answerpreload": "",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
