  {
   "xml_index": 29,
   "xml_name": "L03_029_List Indexing Positive",
   "display_name": "List Indexing Positive",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>We briefly saw lists in class: a <b>list</b> is a single variable that holds a whole sequence of values, written between square brackets and separated by commas.</p>\n<pre>Lst = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50]</pre>\n<p>Each value in a list has a <b>position</b>, called its <b>index</b>. Indexing in Python starts at <b>0</b>, not 1! So <code>Lst[0]</code> is the first element (<code>10</code>), <code>Lst[1]</code> is the second (<code>20</code>), and so on, up to <code>Lst[4]</code> which is the last element (<code>50</code>) in this 5-element list.</p>\n<pre>Lst = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50]\ni = int(input())\nprint(Lst[i])</pre>\n<p><b>Trace through:</b> if the user enters <code>2</code>, we print <code>Lst[2]</code>, which is <code>30</code> (the <i>third</i> element, since counting starts at 0).</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> Using the fixed list <code>Lst = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50]</code>, read a whole number index <code>i</code> (between 0 and 4) and print the element at that position.</p>\n<p><b>Example 1:</b> Input: <pre>2</pre> Output: <pre>30</pre></p>\n<p><b>Example 2 (edge case - first element, index 0):</b> Input: <pre>0</pre> Output: <pre>10</pre></p>",
   "answerpreload": "",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
