  {
   "xml_index": 30,
   "xml_name": "L03_030_List Indexing Negative",
   "display_name": "List Indexing Negative",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Python also lets you index a list from the <b>end</b>, using <b>negative</b> indices. <code>Lst[-1]</code> is always the <b>last</b> element, <code>Lst[-2]</code> is the second-to-last, and so on.</p>\n<pre>Lst = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50]\nprint(Lst[-1])  # 50 (last element)\nprint(Lst[-2])  # 40 (second-to-last)</pre>\n<p>This is very handy when you want \"the last item\" or \"the second-to-last item\" without needing to know exactly how long the list is.</p>\n<pre>Lst = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50]\ni = int(input())\nprint(Lst[i])</pre>\n<p><b>Trace through:</b> if the user enters <code>-3</code>, we print <code>Lst[-3]</code>. Counting from the end: -1 is 50, -2 is 40, -3 is 30. So the output is <code>30</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> Using the fixed list <code>Lst = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50]</code>, read a whole number index <code>i</code> (a negative number, from -5 to -1) and print the element at that position.</p>\n<p><b>Example 1:</b> Input: <pre>-3</pre> Output: <pre>30</pre></p>\n<p><b>Example 2 (edge case - last element):</b> Input: <pre>-1</pre> Output: <pre>50</pre></p>",
