  {
   "xml_index": 31,
   "xml_name": "L03_031_List Membership In Operator",
   "display_name": "List Membership In Operator",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Python's <code>in</code> operator lets you check whether a value exists <b>anywhere</b> inside a list, without needing to know its position.</p>\n<pre>Lst = [3, 7, 2, 9, 4]\nif 7 in Lst:\n    print(\"Found\")\nelse:\n    print(\"Not Found\")</pre>\n<p><code>7 in Lst</code> is a condition, just like <code>n &gt; 0</code> was - it evaluates to True if <code>7</code> appears somewhere in <code>Lst</code>, and False otherwise. Here, since <code>7</code> is indeed in the list, this prints <code>Found</code>.</p>\n<pre>Lst = [3, 7, 2, 9, 4]\nx = int(input())\nif x in Lst:\n    print(\"Found\")\nelse:\n    print(\"Not Found\")</pre>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> Using the fixed list <code>Lst = [3, 7, 2, 9, 4]</code>, read a whole number <code>x</code> and print <code>Found</code> if it appears in the list, or <code>Not Found</code> otherwise.</p>\n<p><b>Example 1:</b> Input: <pre>9</pre> Output: <pre>Found</pre></p>\n<p><b>Example 2:</b> Input: <pre>100</pre> Output: <pre>Not Found</pre></p>",
