  {
   "xml_index": 33,
   "xml_name": "L03_033_Print Elements Greater Than X",
   "display_name": "Print Elements Greater Than X",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Let's use a <code>for</code> loop to visit every element in a list, one at a time - this is one of the most common things you'll do with lists.</p>\n<pre>Lst = [4, 15, 8, 23, 6, 19]\nfor value in Lst:\n    print(value)</pre>\n<p>Notice something different here: <code>for value in Lst</code> doesn't use <code>range()</code> at all! It directly walks through the list, putting each element (not each index) into <code>value</code>, one at a time. This is Python's most natural way to process every item in a list.</p>\n<p>Let's combine this with an <code>if</code>, to only print elements that satisfy a condition:</p>\n<pre>Lst = [4, 15, 8, 23, 6, 19]\nx = int(input())\nfor value in Lst:\n    if value > x:\n        print(value)</pre>\n<p><b>Trace through:</b> if <code>x = 10</code>, we check each element: 4 (skip), 15 (print), 8 (skip), 23 (print), 6 (skip), 19 (print). Output: <code>15, 23, 19</code>, each on its own line, in their original list order.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> Using the fixed list <code>Lst = [4, 15, 8, 23, 6, 19]</code>, read a whole number <code>x</code> and print every element of the list that is greater than <code>x</code>, each on its own line, in their original order.</p>\n<p><b>Example:</b> Input: <pre>10</pre> Output: <pre>15\n23\n19</pre></p>\n<p><b>Edge case (x bigger than everything - nothing printed):</b> Input: <pre>100</pre> Output: <i>(nothing printed)</i></p>",
   "answerpreload": "",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
