  {
   "xml_index": 34,
   "xml_name": "L03_034_Print From Index Using While",
   "display_name": "Print From Index Using While",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Lists can also be walked through with a <code>while</code> loop, using indices explicitly - this is more work than <code>for value in Lst</code>, but gives you full control over exactly where to start and stop.</p>\n<pre>Lst = [2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12]\nstart = int(input())\ni = start\nwhile i < len(Lst):\n    print(Lst[i])\n    i = i + 1</pre>\n<p>Here, <code>len(Lst)</code> is a new function - it tells us how many elements are in the list (here, <code>6</code>). We start our index <code>i</code> at whatever position the user gives us, and keep printing and incrementing until <code>i</code> reaches <code>len(Lst)</code> (at which point there are no more valid positions left).</p>\n<p><b>Trace through:</b> if <code>start = 3</code>: we print <code>Lst[3]=8</code>, then <code>Lst[4]=10</code>, then <code>Lst[5]=12</code>, then stop since <code>i=6</code> is no longer less than <code>len(Lst)=6</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> Using the fixed list <code>Lst = [2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12]</code>, read a whole number <code>start</code> (between 0 and 5) and print every element from that index to the end of the list, each on its own line.</p>\n<p><b>Example:</b> Input: <pre>3</pre> Output: <pre>8\n10\n12</pre></p>\n<p><b>Edge case (start at the very last index - only one element):</b> Input: <pre>5</pre> Output: <pre>12</pre></p>",
