   "xml_index": 39,
   "xml_name": "L03_039_Maximum Element In List",
   "display_name": "Maximum Element In List",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Let's find the <strong>largest</strong> value in a list using a loop - a very classic and useful pattern. The idea: keep a \"best so far\" variable, initialize it with the list's first element, then compare every other element against it, updating whenever we find something bigger.</p>\n<pre>Lst = [7, 23, 4, 19, 31, 8, 15]\nlargest = Lst[0]\nfor value in Lst:\n    if value &gt; largest:\n        largest = value\nprint(largest)</pre>\n<p>We start by assuming the first element (<code>Lst[0]</code>) is the largest we've seen so far. Then, as we scan through <strong>every</strong> element (including that first one again - which is harmless, since it can't be greater than itself), we update <code>largest</code> whenever we find something bigger.</p>\n<p><strong>Trace through:</strong> starting with <code>largest = 7</code>. We check 7 (no change), 23 (update to 23), 4 (no change), 19 (no change, 19&lt;23), 31 (update to 31), 8 (no change), 15 (no change). Final answer: <code>31</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Your task:</strong> Using the fixed list <code>Lst = [7, 23, 4, 19, 31, 8, 15]</code>, find and print the largest element using a loop, exactly as shown above.</p>\n<p><strong>Example:</strong> Output:</p>\n<pre>31</pre>\n<p></p>",
   "answerpreload": "# Do not change this list\nLst = [7, 23, 4, 19, 31, 8, 15]\n# write your code below to find and print the largets value, using a while loop",
   "mark": 1.0,
