  {
   "xml_index": 40,
   "xml_name": "L03_040_Manual Membership Search Using Loop",
   "display_name": "Manual Membership Search Using Loop",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>For our final question, let's write our own version of the <code>in</code> operator using a loop, to see exactly how Python must be checking membership behind the scenes. This ties together everything from this lab: a loop, a flag variable, and a comparison.</p>\n<pre>Lst = [6, 19, 3, 27, 11, 8]\nx = int(input())\nfound = False\nfor value in Lst:\n    if value == x:\n        found = True\nif found:\n    print(\"Found\")\nelse:\n    print(\"Not Found\")</pre>\n<p>We start by assuming <code>x</code> has <b>not</b> been found (<code>found = False</code>). As we scan through every element, if we ever spot a match, we set <code>found = True</code> - and we keep scanning the rest of the list anyway (there's no way to \"stop early\" yet, since we haven't learned <code>break</code>). After the loop finishes, <code>found</code> tells us the final answer.</p>\n<p>This produces exactly the same result as writing <code>if x in Lst:</code>, just spelled out step by step.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> Using the fixed list <code>Lst = [6, 19, 3, 27, 11, 8]</code>, read a whole number <code>x</code> and print <code>Found</code> or <code>Not Found</code> using the manual loop-and-flag technique shown above (do not use the <code>in</code> operator directly).</p>\n<p><b>Example 1:</b> Input: <pre>27</pre> Output: <pre>Found</pre></p>\n<p><b>Example 2 (edge case - value not present):</b> Input: <pre>100</pre> Output: <pre>Not Found</pre></p>",
   "answerpreload": "",
   "mark": 1.0,
